   "text": {
    "L1": "The lowest common multiple is the smallest common multiple after zero.",
    "L2": "A common multiple appears in both times tables. The LCM cannot be smaller than the larger number.",
    "L3": "For 12 and 18, write prime factors: 12 is two twos and one three, while 18 is one two and two threes.",
    "L4": "Take every prime as many times as it appears most often. That gives two twos and two threes.",
    "L5": "So the LCM is 2 times 2 times 3 times 3, which is 36.",
    "L6": "The product is not always the LCM. For 4 and 6, 12 is already common, so it is lower than 24.",
    "L7": "Use the LCM when repeating events meet again. Use the HCF when cutting into the largest equal pieces.",
    "L8": "Now choose the first place where the 4s and 6s meet after zero."
   },
